<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The posets <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖵</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{3}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{3}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Reuter proved <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(P\times P)\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(P\times P)\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> whenever
<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the classification of <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible posets,
Felsner et al. later gave another proof of Reuter’s
result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. They also proved that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(C_{k}\times C_{\ell})=4\quad\text{for }k,\ell\geq 3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(C_{k}\times C_{\ell})=4\quad\text{for }k,\ell\geq 3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After passing to <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible subposets, the problem therefore
naturally divides into three cases: crown–crown, crown–noncrown, and
noncrown–noncrown. The crown–crown case is settled by the result
above. For the noncrown–noncrown case, Felsner et al. observed that
every noncrown member of the classification contains a copy of
<math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{3}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{3}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and proved the required lower bound for</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{3}\times Z_{3},\qquad Z_{3}\times Z_{3}^{d},\qquad Z_{3}\times C_{2},\qquad C_{2}\times C_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{3}\times Z_{3},\qquad Z_{3}\times Z_{3}^{d},\qquad Z_{3}\times C_{2},\qquad C_{2}\times C_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">By duality, symmetry, and monotonicity, this settles the
noncrown–noncrown case. Hence only the mixed crown–noncrown case
remains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This remaining case cannot be treated by the same reduction. Up to
duality, such a reduction would require</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(C_{k}\times Z_{3})\geq 4\qquad\text{and}\qquad\dim(C_{k}\times C_{2})\geq 4," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(C_{k}\times Z_{3})\geq 4\qquad\text{and}\qquad\dim(C_{k}\times C_{2})\geq 4,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">but both inequalities are false.
Indeed, they proved that for every poset <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X\times C_{k})\leq\max\bigl\{3,\dim X+1\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X\times C_{k})\leq\max\bigl\{3,\dim X+1\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a noncrown <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible poset and <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonempty
proper subposet of <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The general lower
bound together with (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) gives</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(C_{k}\times X)=3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(C_{k}\times X)=3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.4" class="ltx_p">Consequently, no proper subposet of <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> gives the required lower
bound through monotonicity. Thus the mixed case must retain the full
noncrown factor <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Nevertheless, their computer calculations gave dimension four for
every tested crown–noncrown product of <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible posets.
For the six infinite families, the cases <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math> were computed
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Table 1]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We prove the Kelly–Trotter conjecture when both factors have dimension
three.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6 Proofs of the main results ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.</span> <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be finite posets. If
<math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=\dim Q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=\dim Q=3</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(P\times Q)\geq 4." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(P\times Q)\geq 4.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This result disproves Trotter’s conjecture. Indeed, the case
<math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n=3</annotation></semantics></math> would require <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(P\times Q)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(P\times Q)=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Following Felsner and Trotter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, we use the
graph of critical pairs to obtain lower bounds on dimension. For a
poset <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its graph of critical pairs. If
<math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y=3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable. Thus a
non-<math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable subgraph of <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> implies
<math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the proof of the mixed case, we work with
<math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}\times R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}\times R</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}^{d}\times R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖵</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}^{d}\times R</annotation></semantics></math>. For every noncrown
<math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible poset <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>,
we prove that at least one of
<math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathsf{V}^{d}\times R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖵</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathsf{V}^{d}\times R)</annotation></semantics></math> is not
<math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable. By monotonicity, this gives
<math id="S1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(C_{k}\times R)\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(C_{k}\times R)\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each of the six infinite noncrown families, we construct an
explicit non-<math id="S1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable subgraph of <math id="S1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)</annotation></semantics></math>
for every <math id="S1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For each of the ten fixed posets, we verify by
an exhaustive <math id="S1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring search that <math id="S1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathsf{V}\times R)</annotation></semantics></math> is
not <math id="S1.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable. By duality, these results also cover the
corresponding dual posets.
Together with the crown–crown and noncrown–noncrown cases, the mixed
case proves Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6 Proofs of the main results ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, all posets are finite. We write <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\parallel_{P}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\parallel_{P}y</annotation></semantics></math> when
<math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incomparable</em> in <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. A
<em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear extension</em> of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear order <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on the ground
set of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\leq_{P}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq_{P}y</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\leq_{L}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq_{L}y</annotation></semantics></math>. A nonempty
family <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of linear extensions is a <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">realizer</em> of
<math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\leq_{P}y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\leq_{L}y\quad\text{for every }L\in\mathcal{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>for every </mtext><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq_{P}y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\leq_{L}y\quad\text{for every }L\in\mathcal{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimension</em> of <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>, is the least size
of a realizer of <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cartesian product</em> <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> carries the coordinatewise
order. The <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual</em> <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
<math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\leq_{P^{d}}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq_{P^{d}}y</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\leq_{P}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leq_{P}x</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the standard
facts that dimension is monotone under taking subposets, that
<math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P^{d}=\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P^{d}=\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q\cong Q\times P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q\cong Q\times P</annotation></semantics></math>. We also
have <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P\times Q)^{d}=P^{d}\times Q^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P\times Q)^{d}=P^{d}\times Q^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Inc}(P)=\bigl\{(x,y)\in P\times P:x\parallel_{P}y\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Inc</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inc}(P)=\bigl\{(x,y)\in P\times P:x\parallel_{P}y\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A linear extension <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reverses</em> <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\operatorname{Inc}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Inc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\operatorname{Inc}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y&lt;_{L}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;_{L}x</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex10X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\downarrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\downarrow x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:u\leq_{P}x\bigr\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:u\leq_{P}x\bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↓</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:u&lt;_{P}x\bigr\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:u&lt;_{P}x\bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\uparrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\uparrow x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:x\leq_{P}u\bigr\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:x\leq_{P}u\bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↑</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:x&lt;_{P}u\bigr\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{u\in P:x&lt;_{P}u\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The sets of <em id="S2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimal</em> and <em id="S2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximal</em> elements of <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are
denoted by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Min(P)=\bigl\{x\in P:\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x=\varnothing\bigr\},\qquad\Max(P)=\bigl\{x\in P:\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x=\varnothing\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↓</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↑</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Min(P)=\bigl\{x\in P:\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x=\varnothing\bigr\},\qquad\Max(P)=\bigl\{x\in P:\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x=\varnothing\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A pair <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\operatorname{Inc}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Inc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\operatorname{Inc}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">critical</em> if</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x\subseteq\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}y,\qquad\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}y\subseteq\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↓</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↓</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↑</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">↑</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}x\subseteq\mathord{\downarrow}^{\!\circ}y,\qquad\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}y\subseteq\mathord{\uparrow}^{\!\circ}x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The set of critical pairs is denoted by <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Crit}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Crit</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Crit}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. A family of
linear extensions realizes <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if every critical pair is
reversed by some member of the family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Following Felsner and Trotter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, the
<em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graph of critical pairs</em> <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)</annotation></semantics></math> has vertex set
<math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Crit}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Crit</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Crit}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Distinct vertices <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are
<em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adjacent</em> if and only if</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1}\leq_{P}y_{2}\quad\text{and}\quad x_{2}\leq_{P}y_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>and</mtext><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\leq_{P}y_{2}\quad\text{and}\quad x_{2}\leq_{P}y_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\sim\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\sim\beta</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent;
equivalently, <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha,\beta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha,\beta\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge of <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this
case, <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are neighbors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For a positive integer <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, a <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring</em> of a graph
assigns one of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> colors to each vertex so that adjacent vertices
receive different colors. A graph is <em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable</em> if it has
a proper <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is due to Felsner and Trotter
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lemma 3.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=d</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a subgraph of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorable, then
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)</annotation></semantics></math> has a proper
<math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring. Its restriction to any subgraph is also a proper
<math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every poset <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, the map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(P)\longrightarrow\Gamma(P^{d}),\qquad(x,y)\longmapsto(y,x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(P)\longrightarrow\Gamma(P^{d}),\qquad(x,y)\longmapsto(y,x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a graph isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">By (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2 Preliminaries ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is critical in <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if and
only if <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is critical in <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the map is a
bijection on critical pairs. By (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2 Preliminaries ‣ The Kelly–Trotter product conjecture for posets of dimension three" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), two critical
pairs are adjacent if and only if their images are adjacent.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We use Kelly’s notation for the <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-irreducible posets. The crowns
are <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}=C_{n+3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}=C_{n+3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the six infinite noncrown families are
<math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{n},F_{n},G_{n},H_{n},I_{n},J_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{n},F_{n},G_{n},H_{n},I_{n},J_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the ten fixed posets are</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,C,D,CX_{1},CX_{2},CX_{3},EX_{1},EX_{2},FX_{1},FX_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,C,D,CX_{1},CX_{2},CX_{3},EX_{1},EX_{2},FX_{1},FX_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The diagrams below use the labels from Kelly’s classification
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
